WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.719 --> 00:00:06.410
In 1982, Jim Henson proved he was more than
just "the Muppet guy" with an epic fantasy

00:00:06.410 --> 00:00:10.140
about the struggle for power and survival
in a fantastical land.

00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:14.760
Decades later, the story is continuing with
a Netflix miniseries, so there's no better

00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:18.260
time to delve into the untold truth of The
Dark Crystal.

00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:23.990
Perhaps the least popular sketch in Saturday
Night Live history is the Season 1 recurring

00:00:23.990 --> 00:00:25.820
bit "The Land of Gorch."

00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:32.100
It featured fantastical and grotesque monster
creations with names like Scred and Ploobis.

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:36.540
Although it stemmed from the minds of Jim
Henson and company, it lacked that signature

00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:37.620
Muppet magic.

00:00:38.020 --> 00:00:42.980
After one year, all parties involved decided
it would be best to end "The Land of Gorch."

00:00:43.540 --> 00:00:49.040
While "Gorch" wound up a bizarre footnote
in SNL lore, it directly inspired one of Henson's

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:50.740
most triumphant works.

00:00:51.230 --> 00:00:55.560
In the seventies, Henson developed a deep
appreciation for the work of fantasy illustrator

00:00:55.570 --> 00:01:00.910
Brian Froud, an English artist with an affinity
for bringing exquisitely detailed fantasy

00:01:00.910 --> 00:01:02.120
beings to the page.

00:01:02.380 --> 00:01:06.460
Around the time that Henson got into Froud's
work, Froud was thinking about turning his

00:01:06.470 --> 00:01:12.270
creatures into a puppet-based film, so he
happily accepted a 1977 invite from Henson

00:01:12.270 --> 00:01:14.200
to visit his workshop in England.

00:01:14.360 --> 00:01:19.740
Once Froud saw the highly detailed "Land of
Gorch" puppets, he knew Henson was the man

00:01:19.750 --> 00:01:24.600
to bring his illustrations to life, and the
two creators agreed to make a fantasy film

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:25.200
together.

00:01:26.020 --> 00:01:31.500
The Dark Crystal is one of the first live-action
narrative films with no visible humans in

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:32.040
the cast.

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:37.940
It's also one of the few in which characters
speak languages invented by linguists specifically

00:01:37.940 --> 00:01:38.900
for the project.

00:01:39.380 --> 00:01:44.000
Children's fantasy author Alan Garner provided
some helpful suggestions for The Dark Crystal

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:46.400
team based on some ancient languages.

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:52.320
For example, a language created for the Skeksis
was based on an offshoot of ancient Egyptian.

00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:58.150
The one used by the Pod People is a take on
Serbo-Croatian, spoken in parts of the former

00:01:58.150 --> 00:01:58.920
Yugoslavia.

00:02:03.430 --> 00:02:08.160
Producer Gary Kurtz has noted that people
who are fluent in Polish, Russian, and other

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:11.980
Eastern European languages say that they can
recognize some of the words.

00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:16.850
For the sake of realism, The Dark Crystal
filmmakers opted not to create a new language

00:02:16.850 --> 00:02:18.620
whole cloth from scratch.

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:21.100
As Kurtz explained to Starlog Magazine,

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:26.420
"Certainly, it's much easier to make up nonsense
sounds, but you can sense it if it doesn't

00:02:26.420 --> 00:02:28.160
have any basis in reality.

00:02:28.620 --> 00:02:33.840
In a real language, there are certain repeatable
phases and a structure which makes the sounds

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:34.840
mean something."

00:02:35.620 --> 00:02:40.840
The Dark Crystal held its first test screening
in Washington, D.C., in March 1982.

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:44.660
Jim Henson figured the plot would be clear
enough through the characters' actions and

00:02:44.660 --> 00:02:49.380
tone, but the test audience didn't think so,
and neither did executives overseeing the

00:02:49.380 --> 00:02:49.980
project.

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:55.510
They thought they had an expensive flop on
their hands, and that they'd spent $25 million

00:02:55.510 --> 00:02:58.700
on a baffling, impenetrable experimental film.

00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:03.280
Henson had no choice but to follow the directives
of the people in charge and dub the movie

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:04.480
into English.

00:03:04.780 --> 00:03:06.100
"I don't like this."

00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:12.240
Screenwriter David Odell had to essentially
compose a whole new script, with dialogue

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:16.300
that not only told the story but matched up
to the puppets' mouth movements.

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:22.200
Then Henson had to hire a whole new cast of
voice actors, then have editors sync their

00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:23.860
words with the finished visuals.

00:03:24.380 --> 00:03:28.640
In total, it took four months to make The
Dark Crystal's audio track workable.

00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:35.080
In creating a vast and elaborate fantasy world
on par with Westeros or Middle-earth, Jim

00:03:35.080 --> 00:03:39.690
Henson and his collaborators developed a wealth
of background stories and other ideas for

00:03:39.690 --> 00:03:40.580
The Dark Crystal.

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:45.460
There've been many video games, comics, and
books continuing the story of the Gelflings

00:03:45.460 --> 00:03:48.650
and the Skeksis, but a follow-up film never
materialized.

00:03:49.220 --> 00:03:54.460
In 2006, The Jim Henson Company announced
the beginning of production on Power of the

00:03:54.460 --> 00:03:58.480
Dark Crystal, a sequel set centuries after
the first film.

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:03.920
It was to follow a girl made of fire who teams
up with a rogue Gelfling to swipe a shard

00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:06.320
of the Crystal to reignite a dying sun.

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:12.000
Producer Lisa Henson told MTV that it was
based on the sequel idea discussed two decades

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:15.620
earlier by Jim Henson and Dark Crystal screenwriter
David Odell.

00:04:16.220 --> 00:04:20.940
Samurai Jack creator Genndy Taratakovsky signed
on to direct the sequel, which would have

00:04:20.940 --> 00:04:24.780
combined computer-generated animation with
live-action animatronics.

00:04:25.320 --> 00:04:27.080
But alas, it wasn't meant to be.

00:04:27.520 --> 00:04:30.060
In 2012, Tartakovsky told IndieWire,

00:04:30.460 --> 00:04:34.460
"We did visual designs, we did a script, we
started testing things.

00:04:34.470 --> 00:04:38.500
We were always a couple of dollars short,
and it just kind of fell apart."

00:04:39.510 --> 00:04:45.020
In August 2019, Netflix debuted one of the
most hotly anticipated and long-awaited projects

00:04:45.020 --> 00:04:50.700
in recent memory: The Dark Crystal: Age of
Resistance, a prequel miniseries to The Dark

00:04:50.700 --> 00:04:51.200
Crystal.

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:57.160
It all started to come together around 2011,
when Clash of the Titans director Louis Leterrier

00:04:57.160 --> 00:04:59.100
took some meetings with the Henson Company.

00:04:59.560 --> 00:05:04.270
He had watched The Dark Crystal on the Titans
set constantly for inspiration, prompting

00:05:04.270 --> 00:05:07.760
the company to ask him to helm its Dark Crystal
sequel project.

00:05:08.220 --> 00:05:12.360
He was excited about what happened before
the events of the film, prompting the company

00:05:12.360 --> 00:05:16.780
to change course and make a prequel miniseries
instead of a sequel film.

00:05:17.260 --> 00:05:22.260
Filmmaking technology has significantly progressed
in the past few decades, so Leterrier had

00:05:22.270 --> 00:05:26.940
many more tools at his disposal when making
Age of Resistance than Jim Henson did back

00:05:26.940 --> 00:05:28.040
in 1982.

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:33.480
But Leterrier is such a superfan of the original
film that he vowed to use practical effects

00:05:33.480 --> 00:05:34.800
as much as possible.

00:05:34.860 --> 00:05:38.120
"The canvas is much bigger, but the feeling
is the same."

00:05:38.580 --> 00:05:44.100
While some CGI technology was implemented,
it was only to digitally remove puppeteers

00:05:44.100 --> 00:05:46.500
from footage of action-heavy scenes.

00:05:46.780 --> 00:05:51.160
But there's still a lot that can be accomplished
with puppetry, and Age of Resistance is just

00:05:51.160 --> 00:05:54.020
one indicator of The Dark Crystal's long legacy.

00:05:54.420 --> 00:06:00.180
"And now the prophecy is fulfilled."

